bibliography: bibliography.bib
abstract: 'Five pages of Noel Swerdlow''s posthumous *The Renaissance of Astronomy* (2026) set out Ptolemy''s solar
  model:

  the length of the year, the eccentric and the epicyclic form and their equivalence, the equation of centre,

  Ptolemy''s derivation of the parameters from the seasons, and the later values of al-Battānī, az-Zarqāl and the

  Alfonsine Tables. We read these pages deeply. Every assertion is quoted and the quotation verified against the

  transcription. Every assertion is then checked as what it is: a property of the construction as a proposition

  of a model written in the language astra/1, a statement that something follows as a step of a derivation with

  its rule, a number as sexagesimal arithmetic or as a quantity computed from the model, parameters found from

  observations as a determination through the model. Every result is shown with the crop of the page it checks.

  Of 45 assertions, 35 are confirmed, one is a historical judgement that arithmetic cannot reach, and nine

  differ from recomputation. Seven of the nine concern figures that Swerdlow reports from his sources and are

  facts about those sources: Ptolemy''s rounded arcs of mean motion, al-Battānī''s maximum equation, and Ptolemy''s

  eccentricity and apogee, which follow from his rounded arcs and not from the day counts of the seasons. Two

  concern the page itself and are misprints: the sign rule for the equation of centre names the same condition

  in both of its clauses, and the advance of al-Battānī''s apogee is printed as 16;37° where the two longitudes

  on the page differ by 16;47°. The derivation of the equivalence, written out and checked step by step, shows

  further that the parallelogram argument does not apply at the apsides. The reading is a record that runs,

  every item of which points back to its paragraph.

  '
