## Five pages {#sec-pages}

Noel Swerdlow's *The Renaissance of Astronomy* [@swerdlow2026] is a posthumous book of more than a thousand
pages on Regiomontanus's *Epitome of the Almagest* and what followed from it. Its first chapter walks through the
*Epitome* book by book. Five pages of that chapter concern the sun: page 17 on the length of the tropical year;
pages 19 and 20 on the eccentric and the epicyclic form of Ptolemy's solar model and the parallelogram that shows
them equivalent;
pages 22 and 23 on the complete model, its equation of centre, Ptolemy's derivation of the parameters from the
seasons, and the values that al-Battānī, az-Zarqāl and the Alfonsine Tables found later. In the Famulus
collection `kepler-sec` these are scan pages 47, 49, 50, 52 and 53 of the document `Swerdlow2026`; every
paragraph has an address, `fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05` for instance (the register counts the running head of a page as its first block, so the fourth paragraph of this page is `p05`).

These pages are dense. They contain three figures, two formulae, some sixty numbers — many of them sexagesimal,
many of them derived from others on the same page — a construction described in prose, an equivalence claimed
between two models, and a sign rule. A reader is asked to believe all of it. Most of it is right. This paper
asks what it takes to know that, and what is found when one checks.

The method we call a *deep reading*: every assertion on the pages is isolated, quoted, formalised and checked,
and the check is recorded so that another reader can run it again. It was made possible by two pieces of
infrastructure: the Famulus reference scheme, which gives every paragraph and figure an address and a
transcription, and the language astra/1 [@grasshoff2026astra], in which Ptolemy's solar model, its values and the reasoning
about it are written as checkable texts. The deep reading is the use of both on one passage. The model read
here is Ptolemy's; the text read is Swerdlow's exposition of it.

## Method {#sec-method}

A deep reading is a list of *items*, kept as a YAML file (`readings/swerdlow-solar.yaml` in the famulus-astronomica
workspace). Each item has:

- a **locus**, the address of the paragraph (`fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p06`);
- a **quotation**, the assertion as printed. The tool verifies that the quotation occurs in the paragraph's
  transcription (the page XML of the collection), after reducing the transcription's LaTeX markup to plain
  symbols and ignoring whitespace. A quotation that is not found fails the run: the reading is tied to the text,
  not to memory;
- a **kind**: definition, construction, formula, sign rule, value, computation, derivation, or historical claim;
- a **formalisation**, which says what kind of assertion it is. Arithmetic in sexagesimal notation
  (`82;17 - 65;30`). A proposition stated in a model (`proposition solar-eccentric: p1`), which is confirmed when
  it holds over the model's whole parameter space and all its states. A step of a derivation (`step
  solar-equivalence-equal-radii: d6`), which is confirmed when the step is true and is licensed by the rule it
  names. A quantity computed or determined in a derivation (`computation solar-parameters-from-seasons: arcs.e`).
  A sign rule read off a model under a parametrisation (`sign solar-eccentric[solar-eccentric.ptolemy]: c`). Or a
  value solved from a model: the value of one observable in the state where another has a given value;
- the **printed result** and the number of sexagesimal places to which it is compared; the tolerance is half a
  unit of the last printed place;
- an **attribution**: whether the assertion is Swerdlow's own statement or arithmetic (`print`) or a figure he
  reports from a source (`reported`). A discrepancy in the first is about the page; in the second, about the source;
- the **evidence**: a crop of the page image (fractions of the page's width and height), produced by the tool.

`famulus-astra reading readings/swerdlow-solar.yaml` runs the list and writes a report: for each item the
verdict *confirmed*, *discrepancy* (with the recomputed value and the difference), *not checkable* (with the
reason) or *quotation not found*, and the crop. The report in this paper is that output, reformatted for the
page; nothing in the tables below was typed in by hand.

The geometrical assertions are checked against astra/1 texts written from the same pages
[@grasshoff2026astra]. These keep four things apart that the pages present together. The *models* are the pure
geometry of the eccentric and of the epicyclic form, without values. The *parametrisations* are Ptolemy's
values and al-Battānī's, each value with its paragraph. The *superposition* places the two forms in one plane,
as Swerdlow's Figs. 1.2 and 1.3 do. The *derivations* are the reasoning: the equivalence of the two forms under
the hypotheses R′ = R and r = e, their equivalence in direction under R′/r = R/e, the equation of centre, and
the determination of the parameters from the seasons. A property of the construction — "r is always parallel to
the apsidal line AB" — is confirmed as a proposition of a model, for every parameter value. A statement of the
argument — "complete parallelogram OESC" — is confirmed as a step of a derivation: here the step that OE and CS
are parallel, by Elements I.33 (@fig-par).

![The scene of the derivation of the equivalence. Left, at κ̄ = 41°, the state Swerdlow's Fig. 1.2 shows: OE and
CS, in gold, are parallel because ES and OC are equal and parallel (Elements I.33); OESC is the parallelogram of
page 19. Right, the same at κ̄ = 0°: the four points lie on the apsidal line and there is no
parallelogram.](figures/parallelogram-degenerate.png){#fig-par width=92%}

Swerdlow's Fig. 1.2 in motion, drawn from the two forms of the model superposed: the mean anomaly κ̄ runs through a
revolution, and the slider below the figure fades from the eccentric form to the epicyclic. The eccentricity is
exaggerated, as in the printed figure.

{{< famulus fr:astra/solar-fig-1-2 animation >}}

The derivation of the equivalence whose scene this is can be opened step by step, each step with its premises,
its rule and the result of the check:

{{< famulus fr:astra/solar-equivalence-equal-radii argument >}}

## The findings {#sec-findings}

@tbl-reading and @tbl-reading2 list the 45 assertions. Thirty-five are confirmed. One — that the distinction between the real
and the apparent non-uniformity of the sun's motion "is recognized only by Kepler" — is a judgement about the
history of ideas that no arithmetic reaches; it is listed as not checkable, and left to the reader. Nine
differ from recomputation. We take them in three groups.

::: {#tbl-reading tbl-colwidths="[4,5,45,14,20,12]"}

| # | p. | assertion (quoted) | printed | recomputed | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 17 | 365 1/4 - 1/300 days = 365;14,48^d | 365;14,48 | 365;14,48 | ✓ |
| 2 | 17 | 365;14,48^d = 365^d 5;55,12^h | 5;55,12 | 5;55,12 | ✓ |
| 3 | 17 | is 0;6,26^h too long | 0;6,26 | 0;6,26 | ✓ |
| 4 | 17 | 3760/304 · 29;31,50,8,20^d ≈ 111,035^d/304 | 111035 | 111035 | ✓ |
| 5 | 17 | 111,035^d/304 = 365;14,48,9…^d | 365;14,48,9 | 365;14,48,9 | ✓ |
| 6 | 17 | 365;15,25^d = 365^d 6;10^h | 6;10 | 6;10 | ✓ |
| 7 | 17 | 1° per century or 36'' per year | 36 | 36 | ✓ |
| 8 | 19 | on which the sun S moves uniformly about E … | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 9 | 19 | complete parallelogram OESC | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 10 | 19 | so that r is always parallel to the apsidal… | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 11 | 20 | the direction OS from the earth to the sun,… | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 12 | 20 | the apparent nonuniform motion of the sun i… | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 13 | 20 | in both models the directions OS and OS′ ar… | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 14 | 20 | There is, however, a distinction between th… | — | — | — |
| 15 | 22 | The mean anomaly κ̄ = λ̄s − λA | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 16 | 22 | the true anomaly κ = κ̄ ± c about O | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 17 | 22 | sin c = e sin κ̄/(R^2 + e^2 + 2Re cos κ̄)^1… | holds | holds | ✓ |
| 18 | 22 | tan c = e sin κ̄/R + e cos κ̄ | holds | holds | ✓ |
