### Discrepancies in reported figures: roundings

Three discrepancies concern numbers that Swerdlow reports from his sources, and all three are of the same
kind: the source's rounding.

**Ptolemy's arcs of mean motion (page 22, items 22 and 23).** Ptolemy's intervals from vernal equinox to summer
solstice and from summer solstice to autumnal equinox are 94½ and 92½ days, "with mean motion 93;9°" and
"91;11°", "and a total of 187ᵈ and mean motion of 184;20°". With Ptolemy's own year of 365;14,48 days the mean
motion is 0;59,8,17,…° per day, and exact arithmetic gives 93;8,33°, 91;10,17° and 184;18,50°. The first rounds
to 93;9° as printed; the second rounds to 91;10°, not 91;11°; and 184;20° is the sum of the two rounded arcs,
not the rounding of the exact total. These are Ptolemy's figures, and Swerdlow reports them; the discrepancy is
between Ptolemy's rounding and exact arithmetic. It is small — 43 seconds of arc, and 1;10′ — but a reader who
recomputes the page will meet it. (We have not traced how Ptolemy obtained 91;11°.)

**Al-Battānī's maximum equation (page 22, item 38).** Regiomontanus reports that al-Battānī found e = 2;4,45 and
c~m~ = 1;59,10°. The maximum equation of the eccentric model is arcsin(e/R); for e = 2;4,45 and R = 60 that is
1;59,9°, one second short of the reported 1;59,10°. Al-Battānī's c~m~ is his own value as Regiomontanus
transmits it, and we have not checked it against al-Battānī's *Zīj*; one second is within the roundings of a
sexagesimal computation of the sine. (The first version of this paper gave 1;59,8°: the figure had been
truncated where it should have been rounded.)

### Discrepancies in reported figures: the parameters from the seasons

Four discrepancies belong to one sentence. Having given the two seasons, the page continues: "from which he
finds that where R = 60, e = 2;29,30 ≈ 2;30 … and λ~A~ = 65;30°" (@fig-seasons). This is an assertion that
something follows, and the model lets it be tested. The eccentric model has two unknown parameters here, the
eccentricity and the longitude of the apogee, and the two intervals are two conditions on them. The tool
inverts the model numerically, twice (items 26 to 29).

![Page 22, scan page 52, `p05`: the seasons and the parameters Ptolemy finds from
them.](figures/eccentricity-from-arcs.png){#fig-seasons width=96%}

| inverted for | e | λ~A~ |
|---|---|---|
| the arcs of mean motion as reported, 93;9° and 91;11° | 2;29,28 | 65;35° |
| the day counts, 94½ and 92½ days, with the year 365;14,48 d | 2;28,58 | 65;26° |
| reported | 2;29,30 | 65;30° |

: Eccentricity and apogee determined through the model. In both cases the solution found is the only one. {#tbl-seasons}

From the two reported arcs the model gives Ptolemy's eccentricity to two units of the second sexagesimal place
and his apogee to 0;5°. From the day counts, with exact arithmetic, it gives an eccentricity half a minute
smaller and an apogee 0;4° lower. Ptolemy's parameters follow from his rounded arcs, to within what a
computation with a table of chords loses, and not from the day counts. The two cases differ only in the rounding of the arcs, by 27 and by 43 seconds; that moves the
apogee by 0;9° and the eccentricity by 0;0,30. Swerdlow
says of this derivation that it "is so sensitive to errors in the intervals of time … that the exact results
are fortuitous"; the two determinations show that sensitivity inside Ptolemy's own numbers. Ptolemy's steps are
not on the page, and we have not compared with the *Almagest*; these four discrepancies are facts about the
reported derivation, not about Swerdlow's page.

### Misprints

Two discrepancies concern the page itself. In both cases the arithmetic of the sentence does not come out, and
the page image confirms the transcription.

**The sign rule (page 22, item 19).** After giving the two formulae for the equation of centre c, the page
states when it is to be added and when subtracted:

![Page 22, scan page 52, `p04`: the sign rule as printed.](figures/sign-rule.png){#fig-sign width=96%}

> "The true longitude λ~s~ = λ~A~ + κ̄ ± c where c < 0° for κ̄ > 180°, c > 0° for κ̄ > 180°".

Both clauses name the same condition, κ̄ > 180°; as printed the rule is contradictory and says nothing about
κ̄ < 180°. The model settles the matter. In the eccentric model under Ptolemy's parametrisation
(`fr:astra/solar-eccentric` with `fr:astra/solar-eccentric.ptolemy`), c as the difference between the mean and
the true longitude is positive in the first half of the anomaly and negative in the second: the tool reads off
the rule "c > 0° for κ̄ < 180°, c < 0° for κ̄ > 180°". The printed tangent formula, tan c = e sin κ̄ / (R + e cos κ̄),
gives the same sign (its numerator changes sign at 180°), and the derivation of that formula from the geometry
holds in absolute value over the whole parameter space. One of the
two printed clauses must therefore read κ̄ < 180°. Which one depends on the convention for the sign of c, and the
formula fixes it: c > 0° for κ̄ < 180°, and λ~s~ = λ~A~ + κ̄ − c there, since the true longitude lags behind the mean
on the way from apogee to perigee. The misprint is a dropped inequality sign. It matters because the sentence
is the one place on the page where the reader is told how to apply c.

**Al-Battānī's apogee (page 22, item 39).** The last sentence of the page reads:

![Page 22, scan page 52, `p06`: al-Battānī's values as printed.](figures/battani-advance.png){#fig-battani width=96%}

> "Regiomontanus reports that al-Battānī found e = 2;4,45 and c~m~ = 1;59,10°, a notable reduction, and λ~A~ =
> 82;17°, a notable advance of 16;37° in 743 years, mostly from precession".

Ptolemy's apogee, on the same page, is λ~A~ = 65;30°. The advance from 65;30° to 82;17° is 16;47°, not 16;37°.
The difference is exactly 0;10°, a single sexagesimal digit, and no other base on the page yields 16;37°:
al-Battānī's own apogee is given relative to Ptolemy's, the 743 years run from Ptolemy's epoch to al-Battānī's,
and the next sentence computes az-Zarqāl's "regression of 4;27°" from the same 82;17° correctly (82;17 − 77;50
= 4;27, item 40). The 16;37° is a misprint for 16;47°. The comparison of the two parametrisations of the model,
Ptolemy's and al-Battānī's, shows the same difference, +16;47°. It is harmless to the argument — "mostly from precession"
holds for either — and it would pass any reader who does not subtract.

### What was confirmed

The confirmations deserve a word, because they are the bulk of the result and because some of them are not
trivial. The arithmetic of the year holds throughout: 365¼ − 1/300 days is 365;14,48 days, which is 365 days
5;55,12 hours, which is 0;6,26 hours longer than the modern mean tropical year of 365.2422 days; Hipparchus's
Metonic derivation, 3760 synodic months of 29;31,50,8,20 days over 304 years, gives 111,035 days and 365;14,48,9
days; the sidereal year of 365;15,25 days is 365 days 6;10 hours; 1° per century is 36″ per year. Ptolemy's
parameters hold: e/R = 2;30/60 = 1/24, the maximum equation arcsin(1/24) = 2;23°. Swerdlow's error analysis of
page 22 holds to the last digit: against the "correct" values e = 2;6,22 and λ~A~ = 66;16° for −145 and e = 2;5,37
and λ~A~ = 71;6° for 140, Ptolemy's e is in error by +0;24 or +19 %, his λ~A~ by −0;46° and −5;36°, and the apogee
has advanced 4;50° in 285 years, of which about 4° (285 years of precession at the modern rate) is precession
and 0;50° proper motion. On page 23, the Alfonsine maximum equation 2;10° corresponds to e ≈ 2;16; Ptolemy's
mean longitude at the autumnal equinox is 180° + 2;10° = 182;10°, and the model under Ptolemy's values, solved
for the equation of centre at true longitude 180°, indeed gives 2;10°; the mean anomaly 182;10° − 65;30° = 116;40°; the error of
−1;5° against the correct 183;15°; and the growth of that error by −0;26,25° per century, which follows from the
excess of 0;6,26 hours in the year turned into mean motion.

The geometrical assertions hold, each as what it is. Properties of the constructions hold as propositions of
the two models, for every parameter value: the sun moves uniformly about E through κ̄ from the apogee; the
radius of the epicycle stays parallel to the apsidal line; κ̄ = λ̄~s~ − λ~A~; κ = κ̄ ± c. The statements of the
argument hold as steps of derivations: OESC is a parallelogram (Elements I.33, from ES and OC equal and
parallel); the two suns coincide, so that the direction OS is the same in both forms; under R′/r = R/e the
directions OS and OS′ are the same (Elements VI.6 and I.29). Both printed formulae for c, the tangent form and
the sine form, are conclusions of a derivation in the eccentric model.

### What the derivations show beyond the assertions

Writing the argument of page 19 as a derivation, with a rule for every step, shows two things that are not
assertions of the page and therefore not items of the table.

The equivalence has hypotheses. "Complete parallelogram OESC and let C be the center of an epicycle of radius
r = e … on a concentric circle about O of radius R′ = R" gives them in passing. In the derivation they are the
two givens. Lifting either makes the conclusion fail, and the check that no step uses a given it does not cite
finds none used tacitly.

The parallelogram argument does not cover the apsides. Elements I.33 needs the two equal and parallel lines
not to lie in one line, and I.28, by which ES and OC are parallel, needs the two points on one side of the
transversal. At κ̄ = 0° and κ̄ = 180° the points O, E, S and C lie on the apsidal line (@fig-par), and the
two propositions have nothing to apply to. The conclusion is true there as well — the sun of both forms is at
the apogee or at the perigee — but the argument by the parallelogram does not reach it. The derivation
therefore has three cases: off the apsidal line by the parallelogram, and at the apogee and at the perigee by
adding and subtracting lengths along the line (OS = e + R and OS′ = R′ + r; R − e and R′ − r). It is displayed
in full, with every step, in [@grasshoff2026twocases]. A figure drawn at κ̄ ≈ 41° shows the first case only.
This is not an error of the page; it is what the page's one sentence of argument leaves to the reader.

## What the reading adds {#sec-adds}

A careful reader could have found the two misprints with a pencil. Three things distinguish the deep reading
from that reader.

**Coverage.** The reader checks what catches the eye; the reading checks every assertion, because every
assertion is an item, and an item without a formalisation has to say why. The tool runs the 45 items in
seconds. Nothing was skipped as too obvious — and the
sign rule, which one reads past because one knows what it means, is where the misprint was.

**Attribution.** Each discrepancy is classified at once: is this Swerdlow's arithmetic or Ptolemy's? The
seven discrepancies in reported figures would otherwise look like errors of the page; they are facts about
Ptolemy's computation and about Regiomontanus's report of al-Battānī, and they are worth knowing as such.

**Each assertion as what it is.** A page of this kind asserts things of four kinds: that a construction has a
property, that something follows, that a number results from other numbers, that parameters result from
observations. They are checked differently. A property is checked for every parameter value, not only for the
drawn one. A step is checked for truth and for whether the proposition that would license it has its hypotheses
and its configuration. A number is recomputed. A determination is inverted through the model. The sentence on
the parameters from the seasons would pass a reader who checks arithmetic only: there is no arithmetic on the
page to check. It is an assertion that something follows, and it can be tested only with the model.

And one thing distinguishes the reading from a footnote: it is a record that runs. The YAML file is the
apparatus; the report is reproducible; every item names its paragraph, and the paragraph is one address away.
A later reader who disagrees changes an item and runs it again.

## Limits {#sec-limits}

The reading covers five pages, not the chapter, and it covers what can be formalised: arithmetic, geometry, and
the consistency of the page with itself. It does not check Swerdlow's historical claims against the sources he
cites — that Ptolemy's intervals are those of the *Almagest*, that al-Battānī's values are as Regiomontanus
reports them — except where the arithmetic on the page or the model makes a claim testable. The checks of the
derivations test truth on samples of the parameter space and match rules as schemas; they are not formal proofs
[@grasshoff2026astra]. It takes the modern values
Swerdlow uses (the mean tropical year, the rate of precession) as given where he does not state them, and says
so in the item's note.

The transcription is the OCR of the book's PDF with its mathematics in LaTeX; the tool normalises that markup
before matching a quotation, and two quotations had to be written in the transcription's flattened form of a
fraction. The evidence crops are cut by coordinates read off a grid over the page image, not by OCR line boxes,
which the page XML does not have.

Finally, "misprint" is a judgement. We call the two cases misprints because the arithmetic of the sentence does
not come out as printed, because the page image confirms the transcription, and because a one-character change
makes each sentence correct. We have not seen Swerdlow's manuscript; the book was published after his death, and
where the slip entered is not ours to say.

## Conclusion

Five pages of a learned book, read deeply, yield 45 assertions. Thirty-five hold and one is beyond arithmetic.
Three show the roundings of Ptolemy and al-Battānī. Four show that Ptolemy's eccentricity and apogee follow
from his rounded arcs and not from the day counts of the seasons. Two are misprints: a dropped inequality sign
in the rule for the equation of centre, and 16;37° for 16;47° in the advance of al-Battānī's apogee. The
derivation behind the page's parallelogram, written out, needs two further cases at the apsides. None of this changes
what the pages say about the history of astronomy, and all of it changes how far a reader can trust them
without checking. The reading is kept as a file that runs, with an address for every assertion; that, rather
than the two misprints, is the result we would like to see become ordinary.

## Availability {.unnumbered}

The reading is `readings/swerdlow-solar.yaml` in the Famulus workspace `famulus-astronomica`, run by
`famulus-astra reading`; its report and the evidence crops are in `build/readings/swerdlow-solar/`. The
astra/1 texts — two models, their parametrisations, the superposition and four derivations — and the language
are described in the companion paper [@grasshoff2026astra]. Swerdlow's book is
cited by its scan pages in the Famulus collection `kepler-sec` (scan page n is printed page n − 30 in chapter 1).
